  "raw_output": "{\n  \"exercise_type\": \"CEFR English Placement Test\",\n  \"section_id\": 4,\n  \"section_title\": \"Advanced Grammar and Structures (C1-C2)\",\n  \"cefr_range\": \"C1-C2\",\n  \"questions\": [\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 31,\n      \"question\": \"It was only when I had finished my homework that I realized I had forgotten to buy the necessary materials. Which of the following is a more formal way to express this idea?\",\n      \"options\": [\"I hadn't finished my homework until it dawned on me that I'd forgotten something.\", \"Only after completing my homework did I remember what I needed.\", \"It wasn't until I'd finished my homework that I realized I had overlooked something essential.\", \"By the time I finished my homework, I recalled that I'd forgotten to purchase the necessary materials.\"],\n      \"answer\": \"It wasn't until I'd finished my homework that I realized I had overlooked something essential.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 32,\n      \"question\": \"The company will only consider your application if you can provide evidence of at least three years' experience in the field. What is the meaning of 'will only consider' in this sentence?\",\n      \"options\": [\"They might consider it, but they're not making any promises.\", \"They definitely won't consider it unless you meet their requirements.\", \"It's possible that they could consider your application even if you don't have enough experience.\", \"You must meet the requirements or else they will ignore your application.\"],\n      \"answer\": \"They definitely won't consider it unless you meet their requirements.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 33,\n      \"question\": \"Had I known that the meeting was going to be so long, I would have brought more snacks. What is the function of 'had' in this sentence?\",\n      \"options\": [\"It's used to indicate a past action that occurred before another past action.\", \"The speaker is expressing regret for not doing something.\", \"The sentence is in the subjunctive mood and expresses a hypothetical situation.\", \"The use of 'had' creates a sense of uncertainty about the future.\"],\n      \"answer\": \"The speaker is expressing regret for not doing something.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 34,\n      \"question\": \"The new policy will have been implemented by the time you arrive. What is the grammatical function of 'will have been' in this sentence?\",\n      \"options\": [\"It indicates a future action that will occur before another future action.\", \"It expresses a hypothetical situation and is in the subjunctive mood.\", \"It creates a sense of uncertainty about the future.\", \"It describes an action that has already occurred.\"],\n      \"answer\": \"It indicates a future action that will occur before another future action.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 35,\n      \"question\": \"The manager, who was known for her exceptional leadership skills, had been with the company since its inception. What is the grammatical function of 'who' in this sentence?\",\n      \"options\": [\"It's used to introduce a relative clause that provides additional information about the subject.\", \"The sentence is in the passive voice and 'who' is the direct object.\", \"The use of 'who' creates a sense of uncertainty about the identity of the manager.\", \"The sentence is in the subjunctive mood and 'who' expresses a hypothetical situation.\"],\n      \"answer\": \"It's used to introduce a relative clause that provides additional information about the subject.\"\n  ]\n}",
